-# My inboxes
-
-> Alan's inboxes, listed on 2026-08-11 and divided the same day into two groups — those that are active and clear, and those that need definition work before anything can be built from them. Records where he drew the line between the groups, his pull strategy (take what is closest to completion, then pull forward), his statement that selection actually happens at the definition step rather than at the pull, and that Someday Maybe projects sit on the same tier as findings.
-
-> So, lets list my inboxes here, I think that's valuable to have recorded for the strategy. These come in two groups, the first is ones that are active and clear. The remainder are ones that need definition work.
-
-The nine, in the order I gave them:
-
-1. Defined Initiatives
-2. In Flight Projects
-3. My short list in an Initiatives notion doc
-4. Someday Maybe Projects
-5. alanNotes on persona pages
-6. My long list on my Initiatives Notion doc
-7. Domain Intent
-8. Domain Design (when the current design is awkward or seems wrong)
-9. Findings
-
-Two things I added afterwards. Defined **Themes** belongs in the first group — I left it out, along with **Direct Changes**. And Someday Maybe sits further down than its position suggests:
-
-> Oh, I also consider Someday Maybe projects on the same tier as finding. They are not in flight, and they need redefinition before they could be implemented.
-
-## Where the two groups divide
-
-The line was put to me later the same day — active and clear down to In Flight Projects, everything from the Notion short list onward needing definition work — and it is in the right place:
-
-> Yeah, that's right.
-
-So the division is this, with the two I forgot restored to the first group:
-
-**Active and clear — four**
-
-1. Defined Themes
-2. Defined Initiatives
-3. In Flight Projects
-4. Direct Changes
-
-**Needing definition work — seven**
-
-5. My short list in an Initiatives Notion doc
-6. Someday Maybe Projects
-7. alanNotes on persona pages
-8. My long list on my Initiatives Notion doc
-9. Domain Intent
-10. Domain Design (when the current design is awkward or seems wrong)
-11. Findings
-
-## How I pull
-
-> I use a pull strategy here. Take the ones closest to completion and get those finished first, then pull forward, rather than pushing things through.
-
-## Where the selecting actually happens
-
-It was put to me that the pull strategy runs entirely inside the first group — that it is a good rule for *finishing* and does no selecting at all, because everything it can choose between was already chosen when someone defined it. I agreed, and said where the choosing does happen:
-
-> Yes, that is correct. The selection happens at the definition step, when I work with a definer agent to turn an idea into a theme (forgot that one in the first group), initiative, project, or direct change (forgot that one too) depending on the size of the item.
-
-So the definition step is the one that decides, and it turns an idea into one of four things depending on its size: a **theme**, an **initiative**, a **project**, or a **direct change**.
-
-## The scale of the second group
-
-Asked how much is waiting in the undefined inboxes:
-
-> A million things. Not quite literally, because I don't have a million things written down, but there are a ton of them. Just for fun, give me a count of the findings/ folder as an illustration here.
-
-The count came back at **1,803 findings**.
-
----
-
-*Recorder's reading, marked.* Two notes on how to read this page, neither of them a claim of his.
-
-Both the division above and the pull-strategy exchange came to him as questions with an answer already drafted, and he said yes to each. That is a weaker kind of evidence than the sentences he composed himself, and where he was only confirming, the page shows the confirmation rather than putting the words in his mouth. In the division's case he was ruling on a list he had written himself an hour earlier, so there was little room for him to be agreeing with something he had not meant. His reasons for the line are not recorded, because he gave none — only that it was in the right place.
-
-The second is the shape the list turns out to have. These are not parallel queues. Everything in the second group becomes something in the first group by having definition work done to it, and by nothing else — so the two groups are two stages of one line, and there is an upstream pipe that decides what the build pipe ever sees. That was put to him and he agreed, extending it with where the selection happens. Read with the division now drawn, it says something sharper: eleven inboxes, and only four of them are on the side where work can actually be taken up.
